::ALG2_U06_L02_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which sequence is arithmetic? {
= 6, 10, 14, 18#Correct. The common difference is 4.
~ 6, 12, 24, 48#This is geometric because each term is multiplied by 2.
~ 6, 9, 15, 24#The differences are not constant.
~ 6, 18, 54, 162#This is geometric because each term is multiplied by 3.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which sequence is geometric? {
~ 5, 9, 13, 17#This is arithmetic with common difference 4.
= 5, 15, 45, 135#Correct. Each term is multiplied by 3.
~ 5, 8, 12, 17#The differences are not constant and there is no common ratio.
~ 5, 10, 15, 20#This is arithmetic with common difference 5.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What is the common difference of 12, 9, 6, 3, ...? {
~ 3#The terms decrease, so the difference is negative.
~ 12#This is the first term, not the difference.
= -3#Correct. Each term changes by -3.
~ -9#This is not the repeated change between consecutive terms.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What is the common ratio of 4, 12, 36, 108, ...? {
~ 4#This is the first term, not the ratio.
~ 8#This is the first difference, not a repeated multiplier.
~ 12#This is the second term, not the ratio.
= 3#Correct. Each term is multiplied by 3.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What is the 8th term of 2, 5, 8, 11, ...? {
= 23#Correct. The common difference is 3, so a<sub>8</sub>=2+7(3)=23.
~ 20#This is the 7th term.
~ 26#This adds one extra common difference.
~ 16#This stops too early in the sequence.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What is the 5th term of 3, 6, 12, 24, ...? {
~ 30#This adds a constant difference instead of multiplying.
= 48#Correct. The common ratio is 2, so the 5th term is 48.
~ 36#This assumes a constant difference of 9.
~ 96#This is the 6th term, not the 5th.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which explicit rule matches an arithmetic sequence with a<sub>1</sub>=4 and d=6? {
~ a<sub>n</sub>=4(6)<sup>n-1</sup>#This is a geometric rule, not arithmetic.
~ a<sub>n</sub>=6+4(n-1)#This swaps the first term and common difference.
= a<sub>n</sub>=4+6(n-1)#Correct. Arithmetic sequences use a<sub>1</sub>+(n-1)d.
~ a<sub>n</sub>=4+6n#This starts one common difference too high.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which explicit rule matches a geometric sequence with a<sub>1</sub>=2 and r=5? {
~ a<sub>n</sub>=2+5(n-1)#This is arithmetic, not geometric.
~ a<sub>n</sub>=5(2)<sup>n-1</sup>#This swaps the first term and ratio.
~ a<sub>n</sub>=2(5)<sup>n</sup>#This starts one exponent too high.
= a<sub>n</sub>=2(5)<sup>n-1</sup>#Correct. Geometric sequences use a<sub>1</sub>r<sup>n-1</sup>.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What type of model is connected to an arithmetic sequence? {
= linear#Correct. A constant difference corresponds to a constant rate of change.
~ exponential#Exponential models connect to constant ratios.
~ quadratic#A quadratic pattern does not have a constant first difference.
~ reciprocal#A reciprocal model does not describe a constant difference pattern.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What type of model is connected to a geometric sequence? {
~ linear#Linear models connect to constant differences.
= exponential#Correct. A constant ratio corresponds to exponential behavior.
~ absolute value#Absolute value graphs do not represent repeated multiplication.
~ constant#A geometric sequence changes by a repeated multiplier, not necessarily stays fixed.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What is the sum of the first 4 terms of 2, 5, 8, 11, ...? {
~ 11#This is only the 4th term.
~ 20#This omits one term from the sum.
= 26#Correct. 2+5+8+11=26.
~ 32#This includes an extra term.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What is the sum of the first 3 terms of 4, 8, 16, ...? {
~ 16#This is only the third term.
~ 20#This omits the second term.
~ 24#This adds the first two terms twice.
= 28#Correct. 4+8+16=28.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which recursive rule matches 7, 10, 13, 16, ...? {
= a<sub>1</sub>=7; a<sub>n</sub>=a<sub>n-1</sub>+3#Correct. Start at 7 and add 3 each time.
~ a<sub>1</sub>=7; a<sub>n</sub>=3a<sub>n-1</sub>#This multiplies by 3 and would be geometric.
~ a<sub>1</sub>=3; a<sub>n</sub>=a<sub>n-1</sub>+7#This swaps the starting term and common difference.
~ a<sub>n</sub>=7+3n#This is explicit-style notation and starts too high for n=1.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which recursive rule matches 2, 8, 32, 128, ...? {
~ a<sub>1</sub>=2; a<sub>n</sub>=a<sub>n-1</sub>+4#This creates an arithmetic sequence.
= a<sub>1</sub>=2; a<sub>n</sub>=4a<sub>n-1</sub>#Correct. Start at 2 and multiply by 4 each time.
~ a<sub>1</sub>=4; a<sub>n</sub>=2a<sub>n-1</sub>#This swaps the starting term and ratio.
~ a<sub>n</sub>=2+4(n-1)#This is an arithmetic explicit rule.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which sequence has common ratio 1/2? {
~ 2, 4, 8, 16#This has common ratio 2.
~ 2, 3, 4, 5#This has common difference 1.
= 16, 8, 4, 2#Correct. Each term is multiplied by 1/2.
~ 16, 12, 8, 4#This has common difference -4.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which sequence has common difference -5? {
~ 5, 10, 15, 20#This has common difference 5.
~ 40, 20, 10, 5#This is geometric with ratio 1/2.
~ 3, -3, -9, -15#This has common difference -6.
= 20, 15, 10, 5#Correct. Each term decreases by 5.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What is a series? {
= the sum of terms in a sequence#Correct. A series adds sequence terms together.
~ a single term in a sequence#A term is one value, not a sum.
~ the ratio between terms#A ratio helps identify geometric sequences.
~ the difference between terms#A difference helps identify arithmetic sequences.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What is the 4th term of a<sub>n</sub>=3+2(n-1)? {
~ 7#This is the 3rd term.
= 9#Correct. a<sub>4</sub>=3+2(3)=9.
~ 11#This adds one extra common difference.
~ 12#This multiplies instead of using the arithmetic rule.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What is the 4th term of a<sub>n</sub>=3(2)<sup>n-1</sup>? {
~ 12#This uses repeated addition instead of multiplication.
~ 18#This uses one fewer factor of 2.
= 24#Correct. a<sub>4</sub>=3(2)<sup>3</sup>=24.
~ 48#This uses one extra factor of 2.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which pattern is neither arithmetic nor geometric? {
~ 1, 4, 7, 10#This is arithmetic with common difference 3.
~ 2, 6, 18, 54#This is geometric with common ratio 3.
~ 10, 5, 0, -5#This is arithmetic with common difference -5.
= 1, 2, 4, 7#Correct. The differences and ratios are not constant.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What is the 6th term of 9, 6, 3, 0, ...? {
= -6#Correct. The common difference is -3, so the 6th term is -6.
~ -3#This is the 5th term.
~ 6#This moves in the wrong direction.
~ -9#This adds one extra common difference.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What is the 5th term of 1, 4, 16, 64, ...? {
~ 128#This uses a ratio of 2 instead of 4.
= 256#Correct. The common ratio is 4, so the 5th term is 256.
~ 320#This adds a constant difference.
~ 1024#This is the 6th term.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which rule is linear in n? {
~ a<sub>n</sub>=2(3)<sup>n-1</sup>#This is geometric and exponential in n.
~ a<sub>n</sub>=5(0.5)<sup>n-1</sup>#This is geometric decay.
= a<sub>n</sub>=7+4(n-1)#Correct. Arithmetic sequence rules are linear in n.
~ a<sub>n</sub>=3<sup>n</sup>#This is exponential in n.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which rule is exponential in n? {
~ a<sub>n</sub>=10+2(n-1)#This is arithmetic and linear in n.
~ a<sub>n</sub>=4n+1#This is linear in n.
~ a<sub>n</sub>=20-3(n-1)#This is arithmetic with a negative difference.
= a<sub>n</sub>=6(2)<sup>n-1</sup>#Correct. A variable exponent makes the rule exponential in n.
}

::ALG2_U06_L02_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L02_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which explanation correctly connects sequences to modeling? {
= Constant differences support linear models; constant ratios support exponential models.#Correct. The repeated operation determines the model type.
~ Constant differences support exponential models; constant ratios support linear models.#This reverses the model connections.
~ Any increasing sequence must be exponential.#Increasing alone does not identify the pattern type.
~ Any decreasing sequence must be arithmetic.#Decreasing sequences can be arithmetic, geometric, or neither.
}
